From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Wed, 6 Apr 1994 06:03:27 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-4-50] PCR: Smug Nihilism

On 27 Mar 1994, Reilly Jones wrote:

> PCR, if not grounded in subjective purpose, is simply smug nihilism.  Your
> knowledge becomes entirely dependent on the strength of intellect of others who
> criticize your positions.  You pick and choose who you want to be around and
> listen to.  By default, you rule yourself by authority because you give yourself
> over to others' ability to criticize.   This becomes your foundation of
> knowledge, your ground.  You are literally blown around on the wind of whatever
> others can convince you of with no anchor point internally.

Criticism is not carried out solely by others. As far as my own knowledge 
is concerned, it is carried out primarily by me. I do not rely on 
authority to criticize my ideas; I rely on myself to criticize the ideas 
of authority, and to seek out possible refutations of my own beliefs.

My confidence in my knowledge rests entirely on my own desire to correct
falsehoods in it and on my confidence in my ability to find them and
refute them. Clearly, choosing the most comfortable authority and relaxing
is not going to make me very confident of my desire and ability to find
and refute false beliefs of mine. This is an activity that *I* carry out;
it is not an activity that I rely on others to carry out for me. 

> Subjective purpose is the root of knowing what is true and what is false

I thought that just a few weeks ago you were all upset at me because you
thought *I* was espousing "relativism". Or are you contending that
everyone has the same subjective purposes? If not, then you must be
contending that there are multiple truths, different for different people,
which is just plain silly. Different people have different values, but we
do not each have our own private physical universe that follows laws that
accord with our purposes. The nature of the universe, and the accuracy of 
my knowledge of it, is not dependent on my values. I seek knowledge 
*because* I value knowledge, but my values do not determine the *content* 
of my knowledge.

> PCR relies on the abilities of others to determine right from wrong for you.

It does no such thing. I can see how someone like yourself might think
that everyone is incapable of applying critical thinking to their *own*
beliefs, but in this respect, not everyone is like you. 

> How is this different from the Skeptics advice to follow convention since you
> can't be certain of anything?  It isn't any different

The difference is easy to show. Many conventional beliefs are logically 
inconsistent and many other conventional beliefs are contrary to 
experimental data. PCR rejects such beliefs, so PCR is clearly not 
following the Skeptics advice.

> If you really wanted to challenge yourself you would never stop seeking
> criticism from the very strongest intellects around.  This is how Socrates lived
> his life and it is why PCR simply seems like the Socratic method restated.  Most
> people, in fact, stop seeking criticism from the strongest intellects and become
> self-satisfied.

When people stop seeking such criticism, they are no longer in accordance 
with the prescriptions of PCR. Socrates recognized only the criticism of 
logical inconsistency, and he wasn't very good at it, since logic was not 
very well developed in his time. If you want to call PCR a stronger 
version of the Socratic method, please be my guest. Is "Socratic method" 
some sort of insult in your scheme of things?

> You should elevate them because they constitute an absolute unbroachable barrier
> to knowing other people's thoughts.  Even as technology advances, allowing
> closer and closer inspection of other people's thoughts, there will still be
> Edelman's "qualia assumption" barring final broach of the epistemological
> boundary between consensual reality and subjective reality.  This "qualia
> assumption" cannot be broached without loss of self of the individual invaded.

Did you really believe that this paragraph was going to communicate
anything to anyone who hadn't read Edelman? I haven't read Edelman. What I
know about qualia doesn't give me any reason to believe that direct access
to other people's qualia would bring about any "loss of self". I don't
even know what you mean by "loss of self". So if you aren't getting as
tired of me as I am of you, please tell us what the magical "qualia
assumption" is and how it mystically protects "subjective reality" from
this dreadful Big-Brotherish invasion. 

> Objective reality is inaccessible to us due to epistemological boundaries.

Okay, we agree on this. The "epistemological boundaries" in this case 
reduce to biological and physical facts about the way our nervous systems 
interact with our environment, and the impossibility of induction (as 
that term is commonly understood).

> Subjective reality is completely accessible to us and us alone,
> the domain of purpose and value.  

This is complete nonsense, and I have already cited the second-level 
references (in Churchland's MATTER AND CONSCIOUSNESS) to the controlled 
empirical experiments that demonstrate that "subjective reality" is *not* 
completely accessible, not even reliably accessible. You choose to ignore 
this. Fine. Keep it up. Ignoring evidence presented by others destroys 
your credibility just as well as any argument of mine could.

Epistemological access to "subjective reality" is neither more complete
nor more reliable than epistemological access to objective reality. We can
deceive ourselves about the emotions we may be feeling at any given time
(denial of jealousy, for instance). We can hold two conflicting values,
and not be sure which one is more important to us. We can be indecisive 
about all kinds of subjective matters, and this indecision reflects very 
real uncertainty. The empirical experiments I second-level cited above 
even show that we can deceive ourselves about sensory qualia. Expectation 
foils perception, and it foils perception of "subjective reality" just as 
much as it foils perception of objective reality. And the truth of 
everything I've said in this paragraph shows that "subjective reality" is 
a myth, that the states and perceptions we refer to as "subjective 
reality" are just a conveniently-labeled subset of objective reality.

> Churchland's brand of functionalism and his arguments for the fallibility of

The empirical experiments referred to were not conducted by Churchland, 
and have nothing to do with his "brand of functionalism". (Hell, I 
thought he was an eliminative materialist, like me.)   Try again.

> applied to the neuronal somatic selection system and Searle's "no deep
> unconscious" models.  It is silly to argue that I don't know what I am thinking
> in subjective reality.

Oh, good. Experiments are demonstrations, not arguments. Now you are 
sounding just like a Renaissance Aristotelian. "It is silly to argue that 
the Earth moves."

> Analysis is grounded in subjective purpose.  Logical consistency is grounded in
> subjective purpose.  Experimental test methodology is grounded in subjective
> purpose.  There is no criticism on the list of PCR-valid criticisms you have
> previously posted that is not grounded in subjective purpose.  It's foundation
> is subjective utility.

They are not "grounded" in subjective purpose. The Ministry of Truth in
Orwell's Oceania was grounded in subjective purpose. The criticisms used
by PCR are "grounded" in their own hardiness in standing up to criticism
and intellectual analysis. The ultimate criticism is when the universe
kills you for doing something stupid. That's called natural selection. 
That's what epistemology is "grounded" in, because that's the process that
created our minds and all their epistemological apparatus in the first
place. 

> selection.  Memes are not "built-in" whatsoever, no "initial-states."  They form
> within the neuronal somatic selection system through complex multicombinatorial
> processes from "microhormonal" biophysical processes to neuronal attractors in
> neuromorphological space.  Memes are propagated within consensual reality.

Well, that penultimate sentence was certainly a humdinger, Reilly. Do they
form spontaneously? Randomly? What is it in the "microhormonal biophysical
processes" that determines what memes get formed? Could this possibly be
interpreted as an effect of the biological substrate? As an effect of
epigenesis? Or do memes just randomly and spontaneously spring up fully
formed from the biological substrate with no reference to any genetic
information whatsoever? I mean, sure, *now* memetic evolution proceeds
within "consensual reality", but where did the first memes come from? 

Okay, so you're arguing tabula rasa. Tell me, how does a baby know how to
suckle? I guess you would argue that all the things an infant is born with
are mere instincts, and that instincts are wholly and purely
distinguishable from memes. I would argue that the distinction between the
instinct to suckle and uncritically-held belief that suckling will nourish
is very narrow, and that the latter belief is certainly a meme. You seem
to have a fondness for hard-and-fast boundaries that slice the universe up
into a bunch of neat little dualisms, but I think that memes are emergent,
and gradually emergent, from purely biological phenomena. Functionally,
they evolve by processes independent of biological processes, but there is
more to memes than their processes of evolution, and it is in these latter
respects that they are firmly attached to biological or technological
phenomena. 

But you still haven't told me where the baby gets the idea that if one (or
two, or three, or whatever) candle burned him this time, that the next
candle he touches will also burn him. In other words, you haven't told me
where the meme for induction comes from. Instinct? Is it still just an
instinct (and not a meme) when a scientist uses it to come up with a
theory to test after examining a lot of data? If in the latter use you 
argue that it is a meme, then when does it magically transform itself 
from an instinct into a meme?

> smug

The real reason I sound smug to you, Reilly, has nothing to do with PCR 
or nihilism. The reason I sound smug to you is because your arguments are 
mostly gibberish. I sound smug because discussing this stuff with you has 
convinced me that I understand epistemology much better than you do. Now 
how's that for smug?

